         "1. HTTP/1.1: Text-based protocol. Supported persistent connections (`Keep-Alive`), but suffered from Application-Layer Head-of-Line (HOL) Blocking: on a single TCP connection, requests must be processed strictly sequentially. If Request 1 is slow, Requests 2 and 3 are stalled behind it. Workaround: Browsers opened 6 parallel TCP connections per domain.<br>2. HTTP/2: Replaced text with Binary Framing and introduced Multiplexing: multiple bi-directional streams interleaved concurrently over a SINGLE TCP connection! Included HPACK header compression and Server Push. However, it suffered from TCP-Layer HOL Blocking: if one TCP packet is dropped, the entire TCP window stalls while waiting for retransmission, blocking ALL multiplexed HTTP streams.<br>3. HTTP/3: Replaces TCP with QUIC over UDP! QUIC implements independent streams directly inside the transport layer: a dropped packet on Stream 1 affects ONLY Stream 1; Stream 2 and Stream 3 continue streaming with zero delay. QUIC also incorporates 0-RTT connection resumption and connection migration across network switches (e.g., Wi-Fi to 5G).",
