         "1. RabbitMQ: Traditional Message Broker ('Smart Broker, Dumb Consumer'). Routes messages via Exchanges (Direct, Fanout, Topic) into in-memory queues using AMQP. Once a consumer acknowledges a message, the broker DELETES it from memory. Excellent for complex routing rules, priority queues, and transactional work distribution. Throughput: ~10k to 50k messages/sec.<br>2. Apache Kafka: Distributed, partitioned, append-only Commit Log ('Dumb Broker, Smart Consumer'). Messages are written sequentially to disk partitions and retained for days/weeks regardless of consumption. Multiple independent consumer groups maintain their own offsets (`offset pointer`), allowing consumers to replay history at their own pace. Optimized for massive sequential disk I/O, OS page-cache utilization, and zero-copy transfers (`sendfile()`). Throughput: Millions of messages/sec.",
