        ("iv-soft-172", "System Design", "Intermediate", "Netflix",
         "Explain the Circuit Breaker Pattern in Microservices and describe its 3 operational states.",
         "In distributed architectures, cascading failures occur when one failing downstream microservice blocks upstream callers, exhausting thread pools and bringing down the entire platform.",
         "A Circuit Breaker (e.g., Resilience4j) wraps remote network calls and monitors failure rates across three states:<br>1. CLOSED (Normal Operation): All requests pass through to the downstream service. The breaker monitors error rates (timeouts, 500 errors). If error rate stays below threshold (e.g., < 50%), it remains Closed.<br>2. OPEN (Failing / Tripped): When error rate exceeds the threshold, the circuit 'trips' OPEN. For a specified cooldown duration (e.g., 30 seconds), all subsequent incoming calls fail IMMEDIATELY with a fallback response WITHOUT touching the downstream service at all. This protects upstream thread pools from blocking and gives the struggling downstream service time to recover.<br>3. HALF-OPEN (Testing Recovery): After the cooldown expires, the breaker transitions to Half-Open, allowing a small, limited batch of trial requests through. If all trial requests succeed, the breaker resets to CLOSED. If any trial fails, it immediately trips back to OPEN.",
         "Always pair circuit breakers with meaningful fallback mechanisms (e.g., returning cached stale data or graceful feature degradation)."),

        ("iv-soft-173", "System Design", "Advanced", "Google",
         "Compare the Two-Phase Commit (2PC) protocol with the Saga Pattern for Distributed Transactions.",
         "In a microservices architecture where each service owns its own private database, executing atomic transactions across multiple services is a major challenge.",
         "1. Two-Phase Commit (2PC): Heavyweight synchronous protocol mediated by a central Coordinator.<br>- Phase 1 (Prepare): Coordinator asks all participating databases: 'Can you commit?' Participants acquire locks and vote YES/NO.<br>- Phase 2 (Commit): If all voted YES, coordinator broadcasts COMMIT; otherwise broadcasts ROLLBACK.<br>Fatal drawbacks: Blocking protocol. If coordinator crashes mid-flight, participants hold locks indefinitely, blocking resources. Horrendously slow and brittle across WAN networks; virtually never used in modern cloud microservices.<br>2. Saga Pattern (Preferred): Replaces one giant ACID transaction with a sequence of local, independent transactions. Each service updates its own database and publishes an event that triggers the next service's local transaction.<br>Compensating Transactions: If Step 3 fails (e.g., payment rejected), the Saga triggers backwards compensating transactions (Step 2 Undo, Step 1 Undo) to revert previously committed state semantically (e.g., unreserving inventory).<br>Coordination: Choreography (services react to events) or Orchestration (central Saga orchestrator coordinates).",
         "Sagas trade immediate ACID consistency for Eventual Consistency, unlocking horizontal scalability."),

