        ("iv-soft-176", "System Design", "Advanced", "Google",
         "What is a Bloom Filter, how does it work, and why does it have False Positives but NEVER False Negatives?",
         "A Bloom Filter is a space-efficient probabilistic data structure used to test whether an element is a member of a set.",
         "Structure: Consists of a bit array of m bits (all initialized to 0) and k independent hash functions.<br>Operations:<br>- Insertion: To add element x, feed x into each of the k hash functions to get k array indices, and set all those bits to 1.<br>- Lookup: To check if element y exists, compute its k hash indices. If ANY of those bits is 0, the element is GUARANTEED to NOT be in the set (100% Zero False Negatives!).<br>Why False Positives occur: If ALL k bits are 1, the element MIGHT be in the set, or those bits could have been set by a combination of other previous insertions (collision).<br>Applications: Web crawlers (has URL already been visited?), Google Bigtable / Cassandra (checks Bloom filter before reading SSTable disk files to avoid expensive disk seeks for non-existent keys), Chrome malicious URL filtering.",
         "You cannot delete an element from a standard Bloom Filter because setting bits to 0 would inadvertently erase other elements; Counting Bloom Filters solve this using small integer counters instead of single bits."),

        ("iv-soft-177", "Computer Networks", "Intermediate", "Slack",
         "Compare WebSocket, HTTP Long Polling, and Server-Sent Events (SSE) for real-time web applications.",
         "Traditional HTTP is unidirectional (client requests, server responds). Modern real-time apps require server-to-client push capabilities.",
         "1. HTTP Long Polling: Client sends an HTTP request; server holds the connection open until new data is available or a timeout occurs. Once data is sent, the connection closes, and client immediately opens a new request. Simple, works through firewalls, but incurs massive HTTP header overhead and connection churn.<br>2. Server-Sent Events (SSE): Client opens a persistent HTTP connection (`Accept: text/event-stream`); server streams plain text UTF-8 events continuously over the single connection. Unidirectional (Server -> Client only). Built-in browser reconnection, lightweight, excellent for live stock tickers, news feeds, and LLM text generation streaming (ChatGPT API).<br>3. WebSocket: Upgrades an initial HTTP connection (`Upgrade: websocket`) into a persistent, bidirectional, full-duplex binary TCP socket. Ultra-low framing overhead (2 to 10 bytes per frame). Ideal for low-latency bidirectional interaction: online multiplayer gaming, collaborative whiteboards, live trading desks.",
         "If the communication is strictly server-to-client (like AI streaming), SSE over HTTP/2 is simpler and cleaner than WebSockets."),

        ("iv-soft-178", "System Design", "Intermediate", "Uber",
         "Compare REST, GraphQL, and gRPC and state when you would choose each in modern software architectures.",
         "These represent the three major modern API architectural paradigms.",
         "1. REST (Representational State Transfer): Resource-oriented API using standard HTTP verbs (`GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`) with JSON payloads. Ubiquitous, mature, easy to cache at CDN/browser level. Disadvantage: Over-fetching (server returns unwanted fields) and Under-fetching (requires multiple round-trips to fetch related resources). Best for public-facing third-party developer APIs.<br>2. GraphQL (Meta): Single endpoint (`/graphql`) where the client declares the exact shape and fields of data it needs in a single query. Eliminates over-fetching and under-fetching. Excellent for mobile apps with poor cellular bandwidth and complex frontend dashboard data aggregation. Disadvantage: Complex server-side query parsing, difficult CDN caching, vulnerable to denial-of-service via nested recursive queries.<br>3. gRPC (Google): High-performance RPC framework running on HTTP/2 with Protocol Buffers (Protobuf) binary serialization. Strictly typed contracts defined in `.proto` files, automatic code generation, streaming support. 7x to 10x faster than REST+JSON with 1/3 payload size. Best for low-latency internal microservice-to-microservice communication.",
         "Industry pattern: gRPC for internal backend microservices; REST or GraphQL for external frontend clients."),

        ("iv-soft-179", "System Design", "Advanced", "Redis",
         "How does Distributed Locking with Redis Redlock work, and what are Martin Kleppmann's criticisms regarding clock drift?",
         "In distributed systems, multiple nodes need to coordinate exclusive access to shared resources across machines.",
         "Single-Instance Redis Lock: `SET resource_name my_random_token NX PX 30000` (sets key only if it doesn't exist, with 30s TTL). Releasing requires checking that the token matches via a Lua script before deleting.<br>Redlock Algorithm (Multi-Master Redis): Deploys N independent Redis master nodes (typically 5). A client attempts to acquire the lock on all 5 nodes concurrently with a short timeout. The lock is granted if the client acquires it on a majority (>= 3 nodes) within a time window significantly less than lock validity time.<br>Martin Kleppmann's Famous Critique:<br>Distributed locks based on timeouts are unsafe for correctness if assumptions about physical time hold:<br>1. Stop-the-world GC pauses or network delays can pause a process longer than the lock lease TTL. The lock expires silently, another node acquires it, and both nodes write simultaneously, corrupting data.<br>2. Clock Drift: NTP jumps or leap seconds can cause TTLs to expire prematurely.<br>Solution: Fencing Tokens. Every granted lock comes with a strictly monotonically increasing counter (1, 2, 3...). The storage service rejects any write carrying a fencing token lower than the highest token observed.",
         "Rule: Use distributed locks for efficiency/deduplication where rare collisions are tolerable; use strong consensus engines (Zookeeper, etcd) with fencing tokens for absolute data correctness."),

        ("iv-soft-180", "System Design", "Intermediate", "Datadog",
         "Explain the 3 Pillars of Observability in Microservices: Logs, Metrics, and Distributed Tracing.",
         "Observability measures how well internal system states can be inferred from external outputs in complex distributed microservices.",
         "1. Metrics: Numerically aggregated timeseries data measuring system performance over intervals (e.g., CPU utilization, memory, error rates, p99 request latency). Stored in timeseries databases (Prometheus, InfluxDB). Cheap to collect and retain for months; powers real-time alerting dashboards and auto-scaling rules.<br>2. Logs: Discrete, timestamped event records emitted by application code (e.g., `User 42 logged in`, `Database timeout at line 45`). High cardinality and rich context. Essential for debugging specific failures after an incident occurs. Expensive to store and search at terabyte scale (ELK Stack, Grafana Loki).<br>3. Distributed Tracing: Tracks the end-to-end journey of a single user request as it traverses dozens of independent microservices over the network. Uses OpenTelemetry / Jaeger standards: injects a unique `Trace-ID` and parent `Span-ID` into HTTP/gRPC headers. Generates a waterfall flame graph revealing the exact microservice causing latency bottlenecks.",
         "In an incident: Metrics trigger the alert; Tracing identifies the culprit service; Logs diagnose the root cause."),

        ("iv-soft-181", "Computer Networks", "Fresher", "Palo Alto Networks",
         "Explain the difference between Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) and Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) and how to defend against them.",
         "Both are fundamental web application security vulnerabilities classified in the OWASP Top 10.",
         "1. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS): An attacker injects malicious JavaScript into a trusted web application. When an innocent user views the page, the browser executes the script in the user's security context, stealing session cookies, auth tokens, or redirecting to phishing sites.<br>- Defenses: Context-aware HTML escaping, Content Security Policy (CSP) HTTP headers, storing session tokens in `HttpOnly` cookies (preventing JS from reading `document.cookie`).<br>2. Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF): An attacker tricks an authenticated user's browser into executing an unauthorized HTTP request to a vulnerable site where the user is currently logged in. Because browsers automatically attach session cookies to cross-origin requests, the target server mistakenly believes the user initiated the action.<br>- Defenses: (a) `SameSite=Lax` or `SameSite=Strict` cookie attributes (prevents cookies from being attached to third-party cross-site requests). (b) Anti-CSRF Synchronizer Tokens (unique cryptographically random token injected into HTML forms and validated on POST).",
         "Remember: XSS exploits the user's trust in a website; CSRF exploits a website's trust in a user's browser."),

        ("iv-soft-182", "System Design", "Advanced", "Amazon",
         "What is Eventual Consistency and how do Conflict-Free Replicated Data Types (CRDTs) achieve automatic convergence?",
         "In distributed AP systems (like Amazon DynamoDB or collaborative apps like Google Docs / Figma), network partitions allow replicas to accept conflicting local writes concurrently.",
         "Eventual Consistency guarantees that if no further updates are made, all replicas will eventually converge to the exact same state.<br>Naive Resolution: Last-Write-Wins (LWW) uses physical wall-clock timestamps; prone to losing valid data due to clock skew.<br>CRDTs (Conflict-Free Replicated Data Types): Mathematically proven data structures that can be replicated across multiple nodes where concurrent updates can be merged deterministically WITHOUT central coordination or locking.<br>- Operation-based or State-based (Join-Semilattices): The merge function is Commutative (`A + B = B + A`), Associative (`(A + B) + C = A + (B + C)`), and Idempotent (`A + A = A`).<br>Examples: PN-Counters (positive-negative distributed counters), Observed-Removed Sets (OR-Sets), collaborative text editing engines (Yjs, Automerge).",
         "CRDTs are the mathematical engine behind modern real-time offline-first collaborative software."),

