        ("iv-soft-184", "System Design", "Intermediate", "Goldman Sachs",
         "What is Database Replication: Compare Synchronous versus Asynchronous replication and explain Split-Brain syndrome.",
         "Database replication copies data from a primary master node to one or more replica nodes to provide fault tolerance and scale read throughput.",
         "1. Synchronous Replication: Primary node writes data, sends it to replica, and WAITS for replica to confirm write before returning success to client. Guarantee: Zero data loss (RPO = 0) on primary crash. Drawback: Write latency is bounded by the slowest replica network round-trip; if replica dies, primary write operations freeze.<br>2. Asynchronous Replication (standard): Primary commits write locally and returns success to client immediately; data is replicated to replicas in background stream. Advantage: High write throughput and low latency. Drawback: If primary crashes before replication completes, un-replicated commits are permanently lost (data loss on failover).<br>3. Split-Brain Syndrome: A network partition isolates two halves of a cluster. If both sides believe the other is dead and both promote a local node to Master, both accept conflicting writes independently. When partition heals, the data is irreconcilably corrupted.<br>Mitigation: Quorum consensus (Raft, Paxos): a node can only be elected Master if it wins votes from a strict majority (> N / 2) of all nodes in the cluster.",
         "Always deploy an odd number of cluster nodes (3, 5, 7) to guarantee unambiguous majority quorums."),

        ("iv-soft-185", "System Design", "Advanced", "Google",
         "How does Google Spanner achieve External Consistency (Linearizability) globally using TrueTime API and GPS/Atomic Clocks?",
         "Traditional distributed databases cannot guarantee serializable multi-region transactions because physical server quartz clocks drift unpredictably by milliseconds.",
         "The TrueTime Breakthrough: Google deployed atomic clocks and GPS receivers in every Spanner data center. Instead of returning a single scalar timestamp, TrueTime's API `TT.now()` returns a time INTERVAL `[earliest, latest]` where the true absolute time is mathematically guaranteed to reside: `uncertainty = (latest - earliest) / 2 = epsilon` (typically < 7 ms).<br>TrueTime Commit Wait Algorithm: To commit transaction T, Spanner assigns it a commit timestamp `s = latest`. The server then intentionally WAITS and delays the commit response until TrueTime guarantees that absolute physical time has advanced past `s` (`TT.now().earliest > s`). This 'commit wait' guarantees that any transaction started anywhere on Earth after T commits will receive a strictly higher timestamp!<br>Result: Spanner achieves full ACID Serializable transactions and linearizable reads globally without central synchronization locks.",
         "Spanner represents the pinnacle of distributed database systems engineering."),

