         "Spatial Partitioning Approaches:<br>1. Geohash: Interleaves bits of latitude and longitude using a Z-order space-filling curve, encoding coordinates into a base32 string (e.g., `dr5ru6`). Strings sharing common prefixes are geographically close. Proximity query: B+ Tree range scan on prefix strings. Drawback: Discontinuities across prime meridian and equator.<br>2. Google S2: Projects Earth onto a circumscribed cube, divides each face into quadtrees using a Hilbert Curve. Excellent area preservation and hierarchical cell levels.<br>3. Uber H3: Partitions the Earth into a discrete global grid of regular HEXAGONS. Why hexagons? Unlike squares (which have 4 orthogonal neighbors at distance D and 4 diagonal neighbors at distance sqrt(2)*D), a hexagon has 6 neighbors all at the exact same identical distance from its center! This makes radius searches, path routing, and smoothing driver demand heatmaps computationally trivial.",
